 - **A referenced alias over a type the resolver can't evaluate keeps its name.** When an alias's expansion holds an `Opaque` conditional, mapped or indexed access type (`z.infer<typeof schema>` is `typeof schema["_output"]`), or an alias already named for that reason, `named.rs`'s `alias_display` returns `Named` with the arguments as written (`StyleProp<MantineSpacing>`), as TypeScript prints it, instead of the source text. Literal unions and other fully evaluated expansions still expand, and so does an object at the top level. `ResolveState::named_unevaluated` carries the "already named" fact up to enclosing aliases; it is taken before each alias's expansion and OR-ed back after (`test_an_alias_over_an_unevaluated_type_keeps_its_name`).
 - **Curated vs Full vs None `HtmlAttributeMode`** changes _where_ HTML-attribute expansion happens, not whether an `InheritedLayer` is recorded — in Curated mode `resolve_component` synthesizes a small curated subset (`notable_inherited`) from `react_types::notable_html_attrs`; in Full mode `extends.rs`'s `real_html_attrs_chain` looks up the actually-merged `@types/react` interface and expands it for real into own props; in None mode neither happens. This is orthogonal to the resolver's normal source-vs-known precedence — it's a pipeline-level configuration knob threaded through `ResolutionContext`, not a resolution-order decision.
 - **React's attribute builtins are props providers.** `AriaAttributes` and `RefAttributes<T>` are read from React's own interface by `html::builtin_attributes` (never from a list in this crate), as `resolve_type_alias_chain` step 2.4: Full puts them in `props`, Curated in the chain's `notable` (the curated subset, `ref` and `aria-*` included), None lists nothing and warns of nothing. `HTMLProps<T>` is the element layer of `T` (`div` when `T` names none) plus `ref` (`ClassAttributes`); in Full it also reads `AllHTMLAttributes` whole, as `extends HTMLProps<T>` does through the interface chain. `key` is never listed, on either path (ADR 0022). A project's own type with one of these names wins (step 2 runs first). `Pick` keeps only the picked members of `notable` and reads from React any picked key the chain lacks, so an unknown key is still reported.
+- **A union keeps what its members inherit.** `resolve_union_alias` merges the members' props and also their HTML layers and collapsed notable props (`InheritedUnion`), so `Omit<A | B, K>` and a bare `A | B` of interfaces extending React's attributes list the element's attributes in every mode. A layer the members share is kept once, and a key is omitted from it only if every member omits it.
+- **`keyof` over a React interface is a key set.** `Pick<DetailedHTMLProps<…>, keyof React.HTMLAttributes<T> | "value">` reads the names from React's own interface (`evaluate::react_interface_keys`, following `extends` into React's declarations), since a props walk lists none of an HTML layer's attributes. `DetailedHTMLProps<A<T>, T>` and similar wrappers take their element from any argument (`react_types::html_element_from_args`).
